<Project>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       .github CONSUMES THE ORG-SHARED BUILD CONFIG IT AUTHORS (.github#609).

       This repo is the SOURCE OF TRUTH for dist/dotnet/Directory.Build.props — the file
       scripts/sync-build-config.sh distributes to sdd / rendering / governance / templates /
       game / audio. Until this file existed, .github was the one repo NOT covered by the thing
       it wrote: none of its own .NET projects consumed it, so ADR-0032's
       DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder never applied here and the F# SDK's library-packs copy
       of FSharp.Core kept leaking into restores. That is not theoretical — it red CI with
       NU1403 on the coordination engine's first push (#608), and each of those projects then
       had to set the property BY HAND to get itself green. Those hand-set copies are gone now:
       the config is inherited, from one place, exactly as it is in every receiver.

       WHY THIS IMPORTS THE ORG FILE RATHER THAN MIRRORING IT.

       Every other repo receives a byte-identical COPY, kept honest by the drift check
       (sync-build-config.sh in check mode), which is a required gate there. .github cannot be a
       plain receiver of that check, because the check covers all three managed files and one of
       them, .config/dotnet-tools.json, pins fs.gg.coord.cli FROM THE FEED. .github builds that
       tool from source and deliberately does NOT depend on the feed that ships it (see
       src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli/FS.GG.Coord.Cli.fsproj): a broken feed must never be able to stop the
       coordination tool being fixed. Adopting the manifest here would trade that property away.

       So a mirrored copy would have no gate keeping it fresh — and a STALE copy of a build
       config is worse than no copy at all, because it looks adopted. An import cannot drift by
       construction: it reads the live source of truth, in the repo that owns it. That is the
       stronger form of the same guarantee, and the honest meaning of "the authority repo is a
       consumer of its own contract".

       REPO-SPECIFIC OVERRIDES GO BELOW THE IMPORT, in this file. The org file's own
       Directory.Build.local.props hook resolves against ITS OWN directory
       ($(MSBuildThisFileDirectory) there is dist/dotnet/), so it is not the hook to reach for
       from here. MSBuild is last-definition-wins, so a property set after the import overrides
       the org default — which is the same escape hatch a receiver gets, just spelled locally.

       (Note for editors, inherited from the org file: an XML comment may not contain a double
       hyphen, so CLI flags are named above without their leading dashes.)
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)dist/dotnet/Directory.Build.props" />

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       FS.GG.Kit, FS.GG.Drivers AND coord-engine (FS.GG.Coord.Cli) ARE A COHERENT SET (.github#2402).

       Three packages that ship from this repo used to carry three independent, hand-advanced
       <Version> literals — one per .csproj/.fsproj. They frequently ship the same behaviour change
       (.github#2135 landed as two separate hand bumps twenty minutes apart: commits f26da6ed /
       d48e1ec2) and nothing compared the three literals to each other, so a bump to one and not the
       others was representable and, twice, happened. This property is the fix: ONE scalar, declared
       once, that all three project files resolve their own <Version> from
       ($(FsggCoherentSetVersion) below), so the three cannot diverge from EACH OTHER by construction
       — a change to one moves this property, which moves all three.

       THIS DOES NOT MERGE THE THREE PACKAGES, their release cadence, or their publish workflows —
       that is a separate, larger, deliberately deferred change (see .github#2402's follow-up; a
       single shared release trigger is a maintainer sequencing decision on the fleet's sole
       coordination-engine distribution path, not a call made here). This property only removes the
       ability for their VERSION NUMBERS to drift from one another; each package still builds, packs
       and publishes through its own existing workflow, at whatever cadence that workflow runs, at
       this one shared version.

       STARTING VALUE. `0.50.0` — one MINOR above `max(FS.GG.Kit 0.49.0, FS.GG.Drivers 0.18.0,
       FS.GG.Coord.Cli 0.23.0)` = `0.49.0` as of 2026-08-11, the highest of the three prior
       independent versions, so adopting the shared scalar cannot make any member appear to
       downgrade. It is NOT `0.49.0` itself: `0.49.0` was already the newest FS.GG.Kit published on
       nuget.org at declaration time, and `check-kit-published-coherence.py`'s PR arm requires any PR
       that edits `src/FS.GG.Kit/FS.GG.Kit.csproj` — which adopting this property necessarily does —
       to declare a version STRICTLY GREATER than what is already published (verified live: the PR
       that first tried `0.49.0` reproduced exactly this red). `0.50.0` satisfies both constraints at
       once. See docs/registry/compatibility.md for the full migration note. Advancing this property
       is now the ONLY way to bump any of the three packages' <Version>; do not reintroduce an
       independent <Version> literal in any of their project files —
       scripts/check-coherent-set-version.py gates exactly that, with gate-inversion evidence in
       tests/coherent-set-version/.

       WHY THIS LIVES BELOW THE ORG IMPORT, NOT IN IT. The imported dist/dotnet/Directory.Build.props
       is byte-identical across every receiver (sync-build-config.sh's subject); it carries no
       <Version> property today and must not gain one on this repo's behalf — FsggCoherentSetVersion
       names packages that exist ONLY in .github (ADR-0039 §5: .github is a producer here, uniquely
       among receivers). This is exactly the "repo-specific override goes below the import" escape
       hatch the comment above already describes, used for the first time.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
