  <!-- 0.50.7 to 0.51.0 (.github#2527): a MINOR bump, and the line change is the point. The three
       preceding cuts (0.50.5, 0.50.6, 0.50.7) were prose and release-notes corrections on the patch
       line. This one carries RECEIVER-VISIBLE BEHAVIOUR: the review protocol admits a case it
       previously refused, so `review` can return a different action for comment evidence a receiver
       already had, and the JSON render of `review` gains a `retiredChains` array.

       WHY NOT PATCH, given Protocol.fs does not move. The "no verb, flag, help entry, render name,
       result name or exit code moved" test the registry rows apply detects MOVED public surface; it
       does not detect ADDED behaviour, so it is necessary and not sufficient. The rule this cut
       follows is the one check-kit-published-coherence states itself: patch for a comment or doc
       edit, MINOR when the change is receiver-visible behaviour, a distinction no gate can draw for
       itself.

       CONSEQUENCE TO EXPECT, NOT A FAULT: kit-auto-publish admits only a same-line patch bump
       (.github#2442), so it will refuse this candidate with `candidate-not-next-patch`. That refusal
       is the rail working as designed; a coherent-set version publishes through .github#2409's
       release path instead, from three sibling tags at ONE commit.

       NOTES AND SCALAR MOVE IN THIS SAME COMMIT, which is .github#2512's requirement and the whole
       reason 0.50.5 is a permanent two-of-three set. -->
  <!-- 0.51.0 to 0.51.1 (.github#2533): a same-line PATCH bump, and the reason it is a patch is the
       rule check-kit-published-coherence states about itself. That item's kit-visible change is
       PROSE: `.claude/skills/pnext-item/references/merge-and-release.md` gains the table of which
       post-merge acts are automated on merge into `main`, and the rule that a pre-act stop condition
       on an automated act belongs in a PRE-MERGE gate. No engine verb, flag, help entry, render
       name, result name or exit code moves, and no receiver-visible engine behaviour is added; the
       item's other half is a new CI gate arm, which ships in this repository rather than in any
       package.

       WHY THE BUMP IS REQUIRED AT ALL, and why it was not required when this branch opened.
       check-kit-published-coherence.py's PR arm demands a `<Version>` STRICTLY GREATER than the
       newest STABLE FS.GG.Kit on nuget.org whenever a pull request edits a `kit:` source. That was
       satisfied for free while 0.51.0 was tagged and unpublished: the tree was legitimately ahead of
       the feed and this change rode into the pending release. `FS.GG.Kit 0.51.0` then PUBLISHED
       during this item's work, the feed caught up with the tree, and the ride-along stopped being
       available. An evidence claim has to hold at the commit it ships with, not the commit it was
       measured on; re-reading the feed before pushing is what caught this one.

       CONSEQUENCE TO EXPECT, AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE ENTRY ABOVE: kit-auto-publish admits a
       same-line patch bump (.github#2442), so unlike the 0.51.0 minor it refused, it will ADMIT this
       candidate and cut all three sibling tags at the merge commit itself. Confirmed by running
       `scripts/kit-auto-publish.py` over the post-merge facts rather than inferred from #2442's
       prose. That makes the release an AUTOMATED act, so this item's post-merge obligation is to
       VERIFY the published bytes against canonical, never to perform the release by hand. That
       distinction is the whole subject of .github#2533.

       NOTES AND SCALAR MOVE IN THIS SAME COMMIT, for the reason the entry above records. -->
  <!-- 0.51.1 to 0.52.0 (.github#2549): a MINOR bump, and the reason is the criterion the entry
       immediately above states about itself. That one was a patch because "no engine verb, flag, help
       entry, render name, result name or exit code moves, and no receiver-visible engine behaviour is
       added". This change moves two of those and adds the third:

         * a new RENDER NAME on the `fsgg.coord.review/1` wire — `state: "acceptedAwaitingChecks"`;
         * a new RESULT NAME on the same wire — `action: "authorizeDelivery"`;
         * receiver-visible engine behaviour: a well-formed, host-accepted chain whose checks are not
           green now reports that new state with `stateErrors: null`, where it previously reported
           `malformedEvidence` with `["review checks are not green"]`. A consumer switching on `state`
           sees a value it has never seen, and — more to the point — a consumer that treated
           `malformedEvidence` as "this chain is broken" was being told something false on every
           ordinary landing.

       It also adds one OPTIONAL input key, `facts.repairAssertionGranted`, read additively: a snapshot
       that omits it parses exactly as before, which is why this is a minor and not a major.

       CONSEQUENCE TO EXPECT, AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE ENTRY ABOVE. `kit-auto-publish` admits only
       a same-line PATCH bump (.github#2442), so it will REFUSE this candidate with
       `candidate-not-next-patch` and cut nothing. That refusal is the rail working, not a fault. The
       release is therefore a MANUAL coherent-set publish through .github#2409's path, exactly as the
       0.51.0 minor was — which means this item's post-merge obligation is to PERFORM the release and
       then verify it, not merely to verify an automatic one. Measured, not inferred from #2442's prose:
       `scripts/kit-auto-publish.py` was run over this candidate's facts and its refusal recorded on the
       pull request, on the same discipline the entry above used.

       A two-of-three set is permanent and uncompletable from a corrected tree — that is how 0.50.1 and
       0.50.5 became permanent gaps — so all three sibling tags publish at the SAME merge commit.

       NOTES AND SCALAR MOVE IN THIS SAME COMMIT, for the reason the entries above record.

       0.55.0 -> 0.56.0 is a MINOR. .github#2561 changes receiver-visible done-stamp behaviour for
       large closure and project-item sets: four previously bare GraphQL windows now refuse an
       incomplete read instead of silently deriving red, green, or NoStatus from a hidden tail.
       This new refusal boundary is observable coordination behaviour, so a patch would understate
       the change and the coherent set requires a real release.

       0.56.0 -> 0.57.0 is a MINOR. .github#2587 changes receiver-visible packed pnext-item review
       guidance: issue and pull-request body path/count/suite claims now require repository/revision
       and command/check provenance before they can serve as acceptance evidence. This is a new
       reviewer protocol boundary, not a correction to an existing description; the engine and
       drivers move only because all three packages are one coherent set.

       0.58.0 -> 0.58.1 is a PATCH, and .github#2571 is the item. The packed change is one correction
       to `pnext-item`'s `merge-and-release.md`: row 1 read `kit-auto-publish.yml`'s TRIGGER and stated
       its ACT, so it told every worker that the coherent-set release is something the merge always
       performs. It is not — `kit-auto-publish.py` admits only a same-line patch bump (.github#2442) and
       terminally refuses a minor, which every coherent-set release is. That is a FALSE CLAUSE removed
       from an existing description, not a new receiver protocol boundary, which is the distinction the
       0.56.0 and 0.57.0 entries above drew when they took minors. The behaviour change proper lives in
       `scripts/check-kit-published-coherence.py`, a CI gate this repository runs and the kit does not
       pack (`registry/repos.yml`'s `kit:` block does not name it), so no receiver's behaviour moves
       with it. .github#2533 — the same shape, a CI-gate change plus a section added to this same
       document — took a patch at 18af7595 for the same reason.

       CONSEQUENCE TO EXPECT, AND IT IS THE OPPOSITE OF THE 0.52.0 ENTRY ABOVE: 0.58.1 IS the next patch
       above both feed frontiers, so `kit-auto-publish` will cut all three sibling tags at the merge
       commit and the release is AUTOMATIC. This item's post-merge obligation is therefore to VERIFY
       that release against canonical, never to perform it — which is `merge-and-release.md`'s own
       patch-line rule, the item self-applying rather than exempting itself. Measured, not inferred:
       the frontier was read from nuget.org and `scripts/kit-auto-publish.py` was run over this
       candidate's facts, on the same discipline the entries above used. -->
  <!-- 0.58.1 to 0.59.0 (.github#2648): a MINOR bump that also SKIPS 0.58.1, and the two facts have
       different reasons. Read them separately, because collapsing them is how this cut gets
       mis-derived as "0.58.2, because 0.58.1 is burned".

       WHY THE LINE IS MINOR. The criterion is the one the 0.56.0 and 0.57.0 entries above apply: a
       patch when no receiver-visible engine verb, flag, help entry, render name, result name, exit
       code or behaviour moves, and a MINOR when a new receiver protocol boundary appears. The
       measurement span is the last commit any receiver actually restores — coord-engine/v0.58.0 at
       12ec83f8, the published frontier — because 0.58.1 reached no feed. Two legs decide it, and BOTH
       are about reachability rather than size:

         (1) RECEIVER-VISIBLE BEHAVIOUR MOVES, which the 0.50.7 to 0.51.0 entry states as its own rule
             ("MINOR when the change is receiver-visible behaviour"). Five defect fixes in this span
             change what a receiver observes from the engine it actually runs: .github#2575 (delivery
             reports a pending check as a CHECK, not a review problem), .github#2662 (a granted
             successor critic can record its verdict), .github#2683 (take walks its own ranked
             candidates after a lost claim CAS instead of giving up), .github#2678 (only LIVE claims
             count as implementer slots) and .github#2694 (no inventory is not an empty inventory).
             Verification: those five commits — 25ce038c, 682dc694, febcb5b6, 1624a011, c8e5ac3f — are
             among the unreleased engine commits `scripts/check-engine-freshness.py` lists at this
             head, so no receiver has any of them.

         (2) THE PACKED KIT ADDS A NEW RECEIVER PROTOCOL BOUNDARY, which is the 0.57.0 entry's exact
             shape — that cut took a minor for "a new reviewer protocol boundary, not a correction to
             an existing description" in packed `pnext-item` content. This span adds the
             finding-packet contract and the independent-review consolidation to that same skill.
             `pnext-item` genuinely reaches receivers: `scripts/generate-driver-manifest` asked with
             `which pnext-item` answers `FS.GG.Kit` at exit 0.

       WHAT THE .fsi COUNT IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. This span also adds 290 lines of public signature
       surface across five .fsi files: a new module, src/FS.GG.Coord.Core/Operation.fsi (154 lines,
       .github#2311), plus additions to Driver.fsi (37), StructuredDecision.fsi (37), Batch.fsi (37)
       and Delivery.fsi (26). Read that as CORROBORATION of legs (1) and (2), never as the criterion
       itself, because on its own it does not meet the criterion:

         * Operation IS NOT YET RECEIVER-REACHABLE. FS.GG.Coord.Core declares IsPackable false (its
           own fsproj) and ships as no package; FS.GG.Coord.Cli is PackAsTool, and a dotnet tool's
           payload is INVOKED, not referenced, so no consumer can open this module. Nothing in
           src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli references it either — it is slice 1 of 6 of .github#2311, and that
           fsproj says so itself: "Nothing compiled after it references it yet; slices 2 to 6 are its
           first callers." Public to the F# compiler is not the same fact as reachable by a receiver,
           and only the second one this criterion asks about.
         * ONLY Operation.fsi IS NEW. Batch.fsi, Delivery.fsi, Driver.fsi and StructuredDecision.fsi
           all exist at 12ec83f8 and were WIDENED — Batch.fsi is 264 lines there. An earlier draft of
           this entry called Batch.fsi new; it was wrong, and independent review caught it.
         * 26 OF THE 290 ARE ALREADY CLASSIFIED AS A PATCH. All 26 Delivery.fsi lines land in
           12ec83f8..a415652f, the 0.58.1 span, which the entry above called a false clause removed
           from an existing description.
         * THE WIRE SURFACE DID NOT MOVE AT ALL. Protocol.fs is byte-identical across this span, and
           check-engine-freshness reports 0 of the unreleased commits touching it.

       So do NOT read this entry as licensing "any new public module implies a minor". The 0.52.0
       entry's minor was for two names ON THE WIRE — `acceptedAwaitingChecks` and `authorizeDelivery`
       — and comparing a line count against it measures size where the criterion measures reach.

       Verification (flags named without their leading dashes, per this file's own header note — an
       XML comment may not contain a double hyphen): `git diff` with `stat`, and with
       `diff-filter=A name-only`, over `12ec83f8..HEAD` restricted to `src/FS.GG.Coord.Core/*.fsi` and
       to `src/`, the latter returning ONLY Operation.fs and Operation.fsi; `git show` of
       `12ec83f8:src/FS.GG.Coord.Core/Batch.fsi` piped to `wc -l` giving 264; `git diff` with `numstat`
       over `12ec83f8..a415652f` for Delivery.fsi giving 26 and over `a415652f..HEAD` giving nothing;
       `grep` for `Operation.` across src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli source matching no file.

       WHY 0.58.1 IS SKIPPED RATHER THAN COMPLETED, AND WHY THAT IS NOT WHAT SETS THE LINE. The
       0.58.1 cut tagged `kit/v0.58.1`, `drivers/v0.58.1` and `coord-engine/v0.58.1` at a415652f and
       published NOTHING: all three release workflows died on `release not found`, because
       `release-saga-prepare.yml` was `workflow_dispatch`-only and no operator had run it
       (.github#2654). Tags are immutable (.github#1772), so 0.58.1 cannot be re-cut to carry the
       commits that landed behind it, and the operator recorded the route on .github#2648 on
       2026-08-16: abandon it, bump past it, cut manually. But the line above would be MINOR even if
       0.58.1 had never been cut — legs (1) and (2) are what decide it, and neither one mentions the
       burned tag — so this bump is not "the next number after a burned one". That independence is
       measured in both directions rather than argued: all three defect-class fixes 1624a011,
       c8e5ac3f and febcb5b6 are absent from BOTH 0.58.0 and 0.58.1, so no route to a 0.58.2 patch
       exists, and the signature span measured from the burned tag instead of the frontier is still
       264 lines across four files including Operation.fsi. Verification: both feeds read directly at
       2026-08-16, nuget.org flat-container index and `orgs/FS-GG/packages/nuget/*/versions`; all
       three packages frontier `0.58.0` and NO `0.58.1` on either feed; `git merge-base` in its
       ancestor-test form for each of the three commits against `a415652f`.

       AND THAT IS WHY 0.58.1 GETS NO STANDING ADVISORY. $(FsggStandingAdvisories) warns consumers
       away from versions that PUBLISHED partially — 0.50.1, 0.50.5 and 0.52.0 each put real bytes on
       a real feed that a consumer can still resolve today. 0.58.1 put none anywhere, so there is no
       listing to warn from and nothing a consumer could adopt by accident. It is a three-of-three
       TAG-only gap, which is a repository fact recorded here, not a feed fact; adding it to the
       advisories would tell consumers to avoid something that does not exist.

       CONSEQUENCE TO EXPECT, AND IT IS THE 0.52.0 SHAPE, NOT THE 0.58.1 SHAPE. `kit-auto-publish`
       admits only a same-line PATCH bump (.github#2442), so it REFUSES this candidate and cuts
       nothing. Measured, not inferred from prose: `scripts/kit-auto-publish.py` was run over this
       candidate's facts and returned
       `{"action":"refuse","reason":"candidate-not-next-patch","version":"0.59.0","terminal":true}`.
       The release is therefore a MANUAL coherent-set publish through .github#2409's path —
       `release-saga-prepare.yml` by `workflow_dispatch` first, so the bytes exist, THEN the three
       sibling tags pushed together at one commit. Until that publish happens, `kit-auto-publish`
       stays red on `main` on its streak bound (.github#2435): that is the frontier rail working on a
       minor, the same as it did for 0.51.0, 0.52.0, 0.56.0 and 0.57.0, and it is not a regression
       introduced by this bump.

       NOTES AND SCALAR MOVE IN THIS SAME COMMIT, for the reason the entries above record — 0.50.1
       and 0.50.5 are permanent partial sets precisely because they did not. -->

  <!-- 0.59.0 to 0.60.0 (.github#2645): a MINOR bump, and it is owed because 0.59.0 PUBLISHED. Unlike
       the 0.58.1 entry above, this is not a skip past a burned tag: `kit/v0.59.0`, `drivers/v0.59.0`
       and `coord-engine/v0.59.0` were cut at 41199bd2 and all three packages serve 0.59.0 on
       nuget.org. `check-kit-published-coherence` requires declared STRICTLY GREATER than published,
       so a tree that edits a `kit:` source while still declaring 0.59.0 is refused: merging it would
       leave every `coordination-kit` receiver materializing 0.59.0 whose bytes no longer match
       canonical. This item edits two kit sources, so the bump is a precondition of landing it at all.

       WHY THIS BUMP BELONGS TO THIS ITEM AND NOT TO A RELEASE ROW. `check-engine-release-notes`
       requires the notes' first token to equal the evaluated Version, so notes and scalar must move
       together, and a standalone bump has no content to describe. Placeholder notes for a contentless
       bump are precisely the failure the engine project's own comment records at its release-notes
       property: a listing that announces the PREVIOUS release's break and none of its own. Notes and
       scalar drifting apart is what made 0.50.1 and 0.50.5 permanent two of three sets. .github#2451
       set the precedent in this exact situation, and this entry follows it: the item that has real
       receiver-visible content carries the bump that describes it.

       WHY MINOR RATHER THAN PATCH. Two legs, either of which would carry the line on the criterion
       the 0.56.0, 0.57.0 and 0.59.0 entries above used, and neither of which is a republish.

         * THE `claim` RECEIPT CHANGES WHAT IT REPORTS. `statusWrite` gains a FIFTH value, `withheld`,
           distinct from `failed` (which asserts a mutation was sent) and from `deferred` (which
           promises `flush` will land one); and `converged` stops meaning agreement with the literal
           column `In progress` and starts meaning agreement with the destination the projection
           derived. A consumer reading that JSON observes both. This is a change in what the tool
           reports for you, not an internal refactor.
         * THE PACKED KIT RETIRES A PREDICATE RECEIVERS COPY VERBATIM. The `pnext-item`,
           `intra-repo-parallel-work` and `drive-board` reference pages carried a start-gate `jq`
           predicate asserting `.status == "In progress"`, which every worker pastes as written. This
           release removes it, states that asserting the literal is a bug, and documents the
           `withheld` versus CLAIM-STATUS-LAG distinction that the fifth value makes necessary. A
           receiver whose copied predicate silently stops matching has been failed by a patch label.

       WHAT DID NOT MOVE, STATED SO THE LINE IS NOT MIS-READ. The `fsgg-coord-engine` wire surface is
       unchanged: no verb, flag, help entry, render name, result name or exit code moved. The new
       value is a new INHABITANT of an existing reported field, and the field's documented vocabulary
       is what this release widens. Verification: `git diff` with `name-only` over 41199bd2..HEAD
       matching no file under `src/FS.GG.Coord.Core/*.fsi`, and `Render.fsi`'s change being the added
       `withheld` case on the existing claim-receipt type.

       NOTES AND SCALAR MOVE IN THIS SAME COMMIT, for the reason every entry above records. -->
